          "text": "     1→# CLAUDE.md\n     2→\n     3→This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n     4→\n     5→## Project Overview\n     6→\n     7→`use-ai` is a TypeScript monorepo for building AI-powered React applications with Notion AI-like functionality. The library enables React components to expose tools (functions) to Claude AI via a Socket.IO server, allowing users to control UI components through natural language prompts.\n     8→\n     9→**Key Innovation**: Tools are defined and executed on the client where application state lives, with the server acting as a coordinator between Claude API and client tools. This enables direct access to React state, browser APIs, and local data without backend dependencies.\n    10→\n    11→## Development Commands\n    12→\n    13→### Setup\n    14→```bash\n    15→bun install                    # Install dependencies\n    16→```\n    17→\n    18→### Development\n    19→```bash\n    20→export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...  # Required for server\n    21→bun run start:server           # Start AI server (port 8080)\n    22→bun run dev                    # Start example app with hot reload (port 3000)\n    23→```\n    24→\n    25→The server must be running before starting the example app.\n    26→\n    27→### Logging Configuration\n    28→```bash\n    29→export LOG_FORMAT=pretty       # Development: human-readable logs (default)\n    30→export LOG_FORMAT=json         # Production: structured JSON logs\n    31→```\n    32→\n    33→### Rate Limiting Configuration\n    34→```bash\n    35→export RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=10      # Max requests per window (0 = unlimited)\n    36→export RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000      # Rate limit window in ms (default: 1 minute)\n    37→```\n    38→\n    39→### Observability Configuration (Optional)\n    40→```bash\n    41→# Langfuse integration for LLM observability (all optional)\n    42→export LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk-lf-...   # Your Langfuse public key\n    43→export LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=sk-lf-...   # Your Langfuse secret key\n    44→export LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=https://cloud.langfuse.com  # Optional, defaults to cloud\n    45→```\n    46→\n    47→When Langfuse environment variables are set, the server automatically:\n    48→- Tracks all Claude API calls with token usage and latency\n    49→- Records tool calls and their results\n    50→- Maintains conversation history traces\n    51→- Provides cost analytics per session/user\n    52→- Requires `@langfuse/otel` and `@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node` packages (installed as optionalDependencies)\n    53→\n    54→### Building\n    55→```bash\n    56→bun run build                  # Build all packages (client, server, example)\n    57→bun run build:client           # Build @use-ai/client only\n    58→bun run build:server           # Build @use-ai/server only\n    59→```\n    60→\n    61→Build process: Bun bundles code → TypeScript generates type declarations → ESM output\n    62→\n    63→### Testing\n    64→```bash\n    65→bun test                       # Run all unit tests\n    66→bun run test:e2e               # Run E2E tests (requires ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)\n    67→bun run test:e2e:ui            # Interactive E2E test UI\n    68→bun run test:e2e:headed        # E2E tests with visible browser\n    69→bun run test:e2e:debug         # E2E tests in debug mode\n    70→```\n    71→\n    72→## Architecture\n    73→\n    74→### Monorepo Structure\n    75→- `packages/core/` - `@use-ai/core`: Core AG-UI protocol types and events\n    76→- `packages/client/` - `@use-ai/client`: React hooks library for AI integrations\n    77→- `packages/server/` - `@use-ai/server`: Socket.IO server for AI agent hosting with plugin support\n    78→- `packages/plugin-workflows/` - `@use-ai/plugin-workflows`: Server plugin for headless workflow execution\n    79→- `packages/plugin-workflows-client/` - `@use-ai/plugin-workflows-client`: Client hooks for workflow execution\n    80→- `apps/example/` - Example AI-powered todo list demonstrating the library\n    81→\n    82→### Core Data Flow\n    83→\n    84→```\n    85→1. Tool Registration\n    86→   Client registers tools → Server stores tool definitions\n    87→\n    88→2. User Prompt + Component State\n    89→   User sends prompt → Client includes component state via `prompt` argument\n    90→   → Server forwards to Claude API with tool definitions\n    91→\n    92→3. AI Processing & Tool Selection\n    93→   Claude analyzes prompt + state → Returns tool_use blocks\n    94→\n    95→4. Tool Execution (Client-Side)\n    96→   Server sends tool_call to client → Client executes tool locally\n    97→   → Client waits for component re-render (to get updated state)\n    98→   → Client sends tool_response with new state back to server\n    99→\n   100→5. Final Response\n   101→   Server sends tool results to Claude → Claude generates response\n   102→   → Server streams response to client\n   103→```\n   104→\n   105→### Key Architectural Patterns\n   106→\n   107→#### Client-Side Tool Execution\n   108→- Tools are **defined and executed on the client** (where application state lives)\n   109→- Server acts as **coordinator** between Claude API and client tools\n   110→- This enables tools to access React state, browser APIs, and local data directly\n   111→- **Security benefit**: No backend API access means AI can only see/manipulate what's on the page\n   112→\n   113→#### Component State Management\n   114→- Component state is provided to AI via the `prompt` argument of `useAI` hook\n   115→- The AI only receives state from components using `useAI`, keeping context focused\n   116→- Library waits for component re-render before sending tool response to ensure latest state\n   117→- State is serialized to string (developer-controlled, not full HTML rendering)\n   118→\n   119→Example:\n   120→```typescript\n   121→useAI({\n   122→  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n   123→  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`  // Current state AI sees\n   124→});\n   125→```\n   126→\n   127→#### Invisible Components\n   128→Components that provide tools but don't re-render (e.g., providers) use the `invisible` flag:\n   129→\n   130→```typescript\n   131→useAI({\n   132→  tools: { sendEmail },\n   133→  invisible: true  // Don't wait for re-render after tool calls\n   134→});\n   135→```\n   136→\n   137→Without this flag, the library would wait indefinitely for a re-render that never comes.\n   138→\n   139→#### Multiple Components of Same Type\n   140→Use `id` parameter to differentiate component instances:\n   141→\n   142→```typescript\n   143→function ListItem({ rowIndex }) {\n   144→  useAI({\n   145→    tools: { updateLabel, incrementCounter },\n   146→    id: `Row ${rowIndex}`  // Prefixes all tool names\n   147→  });\n   148→}\n   149→```\n   150→\n   151→Tools are registered as `Row 1/updateLabel`, `Row 2/updateLabel`, etc.\n   152→\n   153→If component has `id` attribute, it's automatically used:\n   154→```tsx\n   155→<ListItem id=\"Row 1\" />\n   156→```\n   157→\n   158→#### Suggestions for Empty Chat\n   159→\n   160→Components can provide call-to-action suggestions that appear when the chat is empty:\n   161→\n   162→```typescript\n   163→useAI({\n   164→  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n   165→  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`,\n   166→  suggestions: [\n   167→    'Add a todo to buy groceries',\n   168→    'Create a shopping list for dinner',\n   169→    'Add a reminder to call mom',\n   170→    'Make a list of books to read',\n   171→    'Add workout tasks for the week',\n   172→    'Create a meal prep plan',\n   173→  ],\n   174→});\n   175→```\n   176→\n   177→**Behavior:**\n   178→- Suggestions are aggregated from all mounted `useAI` hooks (similar to tool aggregation)\n   179→- Chat UI randomly selects up to 4 suggestions to display in a 2-column grid\n   180→- Selection happens when chat opens or when messages become empty\n   181→- Users can click suggestions to send them as messages\n   182→- If user navigates to a different page with different components, opening the chat will show suggestions from currently mounted components\n   183→\n   184→**Implementation details:**\n   185→- Suggestions are stored in `suggestionsRef` map in `UseAIProvider` (packages/client/src/providers/useAIProvider.tsx:274)\n   186→- Aggregated via `aggregatedSuggestions` memo (packages/client/src/providers/useAIProvider.tsx:368-374)\n   187→- Passed to `UseAIChatPanel` component which handles display logic\n   188→- Random selection uses array shuffle on each chat open: `[...suggestions].sort(() => Math.random() - 0.5).slice(0, 4)`\n   189→\n   190→#### Socket.IO Protocol\n   191→Communication uses Socket.IO events with automatic reconnection and binary support:\n   192→\n   193→**Client → Server:**\n   194→- `message` event: Sends messages with `{ type: string, data: object }` format\n   195→  - `run_agent`: Send user prompt with tools, state, and conversation history (for chat)\n   196→  - `run_workflow`: Trigger headless workflow (use-ai extension, not AG-UI core)\n   197→  - `tool_result`: Return tool execution result for a specific tool call\n   198→  - `abort_run`: Cancel the current AI run\n   199→\n   200→**Server → Client:**\n   201→- `event` event: Emits AG-UI protocol events\n   202→  - `TEXT_MESSAGE_*`: Streaming text responses\n   203→  - `TOOL_CALL_*`: Tool execution requests\n   204→  - `RUN_*`: Run lifecycle events (started, finished, error)\n   205→  - `STATE_SNAPSHOT`: Current application state\n   206→  - `MESSAGES_SNAPSHOT`: Conversation history snapshot\n   207→\n   208→#### Promise-Based Tool Coordination\n   209→The server uses promises to synchronously wait for async tool execution across the Socket.IO connection:\n   210→\n   211→```typescript\n   212→// Server creates promise and stores resolver\n   213→const promise = new Promise(resolve => {\n   214→  session.pendingToolCalls.set(toolCallId, resolve);\n   215→});\n   216→\n   217→// Send tool_call event to client\n   218→socket.emit('event', { type: 'TOOL_CALL_START', toolCallId, ... });\n   219→\n   220→// Wait for client to execute and send tool_result\n   221→const result = await promise;\n   222→```\n   223→\n   224→This enables synchronous-style code despite async Socket.IO communication.\n   225→\n   226→#### Conversation History Management\n   227→- Server maintains **full conversation history per Socket.IO session**\n   228→- History includes user messages, assistant messages (with tool_use blocks), and tool results\n   229→- Enables multi-turn conversations with context preservation\n   230→- History is lost on disconnect (session-based, not persisted to database)\n   231→- **Prompt caching**: Anthropic prompt caching automatically used for repeated conversations\n   232→\n   233→#### Tool Aggregation Pattern\n   234→- `UseAIProvider` maintains a registry of tools from all mounted `useAI` hooks\n   235→- Tools from multiple components are merged into a single set\n   236→- Each hook gets a unique ID for registration/cleanup\n   237→- Enables compositional tool registration across component tree\n   238→- Single chat interface knows about all tools currently rendered on page\n   239→\n   240→### Plugin Architecture and Extensibility\n   241→\n   242→The server supports a **plugin system** that allows extending functionality beyond core chat operations. Plugins can handle custom message types, add new features, and integrate with external platforms.\n   243→\n   244→#### UseAIServerPlugin Interface\n   245→\n   246→All plugins implement a common interface:\n   247→\n   248→```typescript\n   249→interface UseAIServerPlugin {\n   250→  /**\n   251→   * Get the unique name for this plugin\n   252→   */\n   253→  getName(): string;\n   254→\n   255→  /**\n   256→   * Register message handlers with the server\n   257→   */\n   258→  registerHandlers(server: {\n   259→    registerMessageHandler(type: string, handler: MessageHandler): void;\n   260→  }): void;\n   261→\n   262→  /**\n   263→   * Optional: Called when a client connects\n   264→   */\n   265→  onClientConnect?(session: ClientSession): void;\n   266→\n   267→  /**\n   268→   * Optional: Called when a client disconnects\n   269→   */\n   270→  onClientDisconnect?(session: ClientSession): void;\n   271→}\n   272→```\n   273→\n   274→Location: `packages/server/src/plugins/types.ts`\n   275→\n   276→#### Built-in Plugin: WorkflowsPlugin\n   277→\n   278→The `@use-ai/plugin-workflows` package provides headless workflow execution:\n   279→\n   280→```typescript\n   281→import { UseAIServer, AISDKAgent } from '@use-ai/server';\n   282→import { WorkflowsPlugin } from '@use-ai/plugin-workflows';\n   283→import { DifyWorkflowRunner } from '@use-ai/plugin-workflows';\n   284→import { anthropic } from '@ai-sdk/anthropic';\n   285→\n   286→const model = anthropic('claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022');\n   287→\n   288→const server = new UseAIServer({\n   289→  agents: {\n   290→    claude: new AISDKAgent({ model }),\n   291→  },\n   292→  defaultAgent: 'claude',\n   293→  plugins: [\n   294→    new WorkflowsPlugin({\n   295→      runners: new Map([\n   296→        ['dify', new DifyWorkflowRunner({\n   297→          apiBaseUrl: process.env.DIFY_API_URL || 'http://localhost:3001/v1',\n   298→          workflows: {\n   299→            'greeting-workflow': process.env.DIFY_GREETING_WORKFLOW_KEY!,\n   300→            'pdf-processor': process.env.DIFY_PDF_PROCESSOR_KEY!,\n   301→          },\n   302→        })],\n   303→      ]),\n   304→    }),\n   305→  ],\n   306→});\n   307→```\n   308→\n   309→Location: `packages/plugin-workflows/src/WorkflowsPlugin.ts`\n   310→\n   311→### Agent vs WorkflowRunner Concepts\n   312→\n   313→The library separates two distinct concepts:\n   314→\n   315→#### Agent Interface (for conversational chat)\n   316→\n   317→Agents handle chat-based interactions with conversation history:\n   318→\n   319→```typescript\n   320→interface Agent {\n   321→  /**\n   322→   * Execute a chat interaction and emit AG-UI events.\n   323→   * @param input - Agent input including messages, tools, state, etc.\n   324→   * @param events - Event emitter for AG-UI protocol events\n   325→   * @returns Result with conversation history or error\n   326→   */\n   327→  run(input: AgentInput, events: EventEmitter): Promise<AgentResult>;\n   328→\n   329→  /**\n   330→   * Get the unique name for this agent (e.g., 'claude')\n   331→   */\n   332→  getName(): string;\n   333→}\n   334→```\n   335→\n   336→Location: `packages/server/src/agents/types.ts`\n   337→\n   338→**AISDKAgent** (default):\n   339→- Uses Anthropic Claude API via AI SDK\n   340→- Supports multi-turn conversations with context preservation\n   341→- Handles tool calls with promise-based coordination\n   342→- Used for chat interactions (via `useAI`)\n   343→- Location: `packages/server/src/agents/AISDKAgent.ts`\n   344→\n   345→#### WorkflowRunner Interface (for headless workflows)\n   346→\n   347→WorkflowRunners handle stateless, button-triggered operations:\n   348→\n   349→```typescript\n   350→interface WorkflowRunner {\n   351→  /**\n   352→   * Get the unique name for this workflow runner\n   353→   */\n   354→  getName(): string;\n   355→\n   356→  /**\n   357→   * Execute a workflow and emit AG-UI events.\n   358→   * @param input - Workflow input including workflowId, inputs, tools\n   359→   * @param events - Event emitter for AG-UI protocol events\n   360→   * @returns Result with outputs or error\n   361→   */\n   362→  execute(input: WorkflowInput, events: EventEmitter): Promise<WorkflowResult>;\n   363→}\n   364→```\n   365→\n   366→Location: `packages/plugin-workflows/src/types.ts`\n   367→\n   368→**Built-in WorkflowRunners**:\n   369→- `DifyWorkflowRunner`: Integrates with Dify.AI workflow platform (included in `@use-ai/plugin-workflows`)\n   370→\n   371→**Example third-party WorkflowRunners** (could be implemented by plugin authors):\n   372→- `FlowiseWorkflowRunner`: Integrates with Flowise workflow platform\n   373→- `n8nWorkflowRunner`: Integrates with n8n workflow automation\n   374→- Custom runners can be implemented as separate packages following the `WorkflowRunner` interface\n   375→\n   376→#### Headless Workflows vs Chat\n   377→\n   378→**Chat** (via `useAI`):\n   379→- Conversational UI with chat panel\n   380→- Multi-turn conversations with history\n   381→- Uses configured Agent (e.g., AISDKAgent)\n   382→- Triggered by user typing in chat\n   383→- Tools registered from mounted components\n   384→\n   385→**Workflows** (via `useAIWorkflow`):\n   386→- No chat UI involvement (headless)\n   387→- Stateless, single-run operations\n   388→- Can use any registered WorkflowRunner\n   389→- Triggered by button clicks or events\n   390→- Tools defined locally in trigger options\n   391→\n   392→#### Using `useAIWorkflow` Hook\n   393→\n   394→The `useAIWorkflow` hook enables headless workflow execution from React components:\n   395→\n   396→```typescript\n   397→import { useAIWorkflow } from '@use-ai/plugin-workflows-client';\n   398→import { defineTool } from '@use-ai/client';\n   399→import { z } from 'zod';\n   400→\n   401→function PDFUploadButton() {\n   402→  // For Dify, the workflowId IS the app API key\n   403→  const { trigger, status, text, error, connected } = useAIWorkflow('dify', 'app-your-api-key');\n   404→\n   405→  const handleUpload = async (file: File) => {\n   406→    const pdfData = await file.arrayBuffer();\n   407→\n   408→    await trigger({\n   409→      inputs: { file: pdfData },         // Workflow inputs\n   410→\n   411→      // Tools that the workflow can call\n   412→      tools: {\n   413→        insertText: defineTool(\n   414→          'Insert text into the document',\n   415→          z.object({ text: z.string() }),\n   416→          (input) => {\n   417→            document.body.appendChild(document.createTextNode(input.text));\n   418→            return { success: true };\n   419→          }\n   420→        )\n   421→      },\n   422→\n   423→      // Callbacks for progress tracking\n   424→      onProgress: (progress) => {\n   425→        console.log('Status:', progress.status);\n   426→        if (progress.toolCalls) {\n   427→          progress.toolCalls.forEach(tc => {\n   428→            console.log('Tool executed:', tc.toolName);\n   429→          });\n   430→        }\n   431→      },\n   432→\n   433→      onComplete: (result) => {\n   434→        console.log('Workflow completed:', result);\n   435→      },\n   436→\n   437→      onError: (err) => {\n   438→        console.error('Workflow failed:', err);\n   439→      },\n   440→    });\n   441→  };\n   442→\n   443→  return (\n   444→    <div>\n   445→      <input\n   446→        type=\"file\"\n   447→        onChange={(e) => e.target.files && handleUpload(e.target.files[0])}\n   448→        disabled={!connected || status === 'running'}\n   449→      />\n   450→      {status === 'running' && <p>Processing...</p>}\n   451→      {status === 'completed' && <p>Done! {text}</p>}\n   452→      {status === 'error' && <p>Error: {error?.message}</p>}\n   453→    </div>\n   454→  );\n   455→}\n   456→```\n   457→\n   458→**Example workflow scenario:**\n   459→1. User clicks button to upload PDF\n   460→2. Dify workflow triggered with PDF data as input\n   461→3. Workflow converts PDF to text (in Dify)\n   462→4. Workflow calls `insertText` tool to insert text into UI (on client)\n   463→5. No chat UI interaction required\n   464→\n   465→Location: `packages/plugin-workflows-client/src/useAIWorkflow.ts`\n   466→Example: `apps/example/src/pages/WorkflowDemoPage.tsx`\n   467→\n   468→#### Configuring the Server with Agents and Plugins\n   469→\n   470→The server supports separate configuration for conversational agents and workflow runners via plugins:\n   471→\n   472→```typescript\n   473→import { UseAIServer, AISDKAgent } from '@use-ai/server';\n   474→import { WorkflowsPlugin, DifyWorkflowRunner } from '@use-ai/plugin-workflows';\n   475→import { anthropic } from '@ai-sdk/anthropic';\n   476→\n   477→const model = anthropic('claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022');\n   478→\n   479→const server = new UseAIServer({\n   480→  // Agents for conversational chat\n   481→  agents: {\n   482→    claude: new AISDKAgent({ model }),\n   483→  },\n   484→  defaultAgent: 'claude', // Default agent for chat\n   485→\n   486→  // Plugins for extended functionality\n   487→  plugins: [\n   488→    new WorkflowsPlugin({\n   489→      runners: new Map([\n   490→        ['dify', new DifyWorkflowRunner({\n   491→          apiBaseUrl: process.env.DIFY_API_URL || 'http://localhost:3001/v1',\n   492→          workflows: {\n   493→            'greeting-workflow': process.env.DIFY_GREETING_WORKFLOW_KEY!,\n   494→            'pdf-processor': process.env.DIFY_PDF_PROCESSOR_KEY!,\n   495→          },\n   496→        })],\n   497→      ]),\n   498→    }),\n   499→  ],\n   500→});\n   501→```\n   502→\n   503→WorkflowRunners are selected by name when calling the `useAIWorkflow` hook.\n   504→For Dify, the workflowId parameter is a meaningful workflow name (mapped to API key on server):\n   505→```typescript\n   506→const { trigger } = useAIWorkflow('dify', 'greeting-workflow');\n   507→\n   508→await trigger({\n   509→  inputs: { username: 'Alice' },\n   510→});\n   511→```\n   512→\n   513→**Fallback behavior**: If the workflow name is not found in the mapping, DifyWorkflowRunner uses the workflowId directly as an API key. This allows for backward compatibility and ad-hoc testing:\n   514→```typescript\n   515→// Direct API key usage (fallback)\n   516→const { trigger } = useAIWorkflow('dify', 'app-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx');\n   517→```\n   518→\n   519→#### Creating Custom Agents\n   520→\n   521→Implement the `Agent` interface to integrate a custom chat agent:\n   522→\n   523→```typescript\n   524→import { Agent, AgentInput, EventEmitter, AgentResult } from '@use-ai/server';\n   525→import { EventType } from '@use-ai/core';\n   526→\n   527→export class CustomAgent implements Agent {\n   528→  getName(): string {\n   529→    return 'custom';\n   530→  }\n   531→\n   532→  async run(input: AgentInput, events: EventEmitter): Promise<AgentResult> {\n   533→    const { session, runId, messages, tools, state } = input;\n   534→\n   535→    try {\n   536→      // Emit RUN_STARTED\n   537→      events.emit({\n   538→        type: EventType.RUN_STARTED,\n   539→        threadId: session.threadId,\n   540→        runId,\n   541→        input: input.originalInput,\n   542→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   543→      });\n   544→\n   545→      // Call your custom AI platform\n   546→      const result = await callCustomPlatform(messages, tools, state);\n   547→\n   548→      // Emit TEXT_MESSAGE events\n   549→      events.emit({\n   550→        type: EventType.TEXT_MESSAGE_START,\n   551→        messageId: 'msg-1',\n   552→        role: 'assistant',\n   553→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   554→      });\n   555→\n   556→      events.emit({\n   557→        type: EventType.TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT,\n   558→        messageId: 'msg-1',\n   559→        delta: result.text,\n   560→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   561→      });\n   562→\n   563→      events.emit({\n   564→        type: EventType.TEXT_MESSAGE_END,\n   565→        messageId: 'msg-1',\n   566→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   567→      });\n   568→\n   569→      // Emit RUN_FINISHED\n   570→      events.emit({\n   571→        type: EventType.RUN_FINISHED,\n   572→        threadId: session.threadId,\n   573→        runId,\n   574→        result: JSON.stringify(result),\n   575→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   576→      });\n   577→\n   578→      return {\n   579→        success: true,\n   580→        conversationHistory: session.conversationHistory,\n   581→      };\n   582→    } catch (error) {\n   583→      // Emit RUN_ERROR\n   584→      events.emit({\n   585→        type: EventType.RUN_ERROR,\n   586→        message: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unknown error',\n   587→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   588→      });\n   589→\n   590→      return {\n   591→        success: false,\n   592→        error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unknown error',\n   593→        conversationHistory: messages,\n   594→      };\n   595→    }\n   596→  }\n   597→}\n   598→```\n   599→\n   600→**Key responsibilities:**\n   601→- Emit `RUN_STARTED` at the beginning\n   602→- Call your AI platform's API\n   603→- Emit `TEXT_MESSAGE_*` events for text responses\n   604→- Emit `TOOL_CALL_*` events for tool executions\n   605→- Wait for tool results via `session.pendingToolCalls`\n   606→- Emit `RUN_FINISHED` on success or `RUN_ERROR` on failure\n   607→- Return conversation history for multi-turn conversations\n   608→\n   609→#### Creating Custom WorkflowRunners\n   610→\n   611→Implement the `WorkflowRunner` interface to integrate with workflow platforms:\n   612→\n   613→```typescript\n   614→import { WorkflowRunner, WorkflowInput, WorkflowResult, EventEmitter } from '@use-ai/plugin-workflows';\n   615→import { EventType } from '@use-ai/core';\n   616→\n   617→export class DifyWorkflowRunner implements WorkflowRunner {\n   618→  private apiBaseUrl: string;\n   619→  private workflows: Record<string, string>;\n   620→\n   621→  constructor(config: { apiBaseUrl: string; workflows: Record<string, string> }) {\n   622→    this.apiBaseUrl = config.apiBaseUrl;\n   623→    this.workflows = config.workflows;\n   624→  }\n   625→\n   626→  getName(): string {\n   627→    return 'dify';\n   628→  }\n   629→\n   630→  async execute(input: WorkflowInput, events: EventEmitter): Promise<WorkflowResult> {\n   631→    const { session, runId, threadId, workflowId, inputs, tools } = input;\n   632→\n   633→    // Look up API key from workflow name\n   634→    const apiKey = this.workflows[workflowId];\n   635→    if (!apiKey) {\n   636→      const errorMessage = `Workflow '${workflowId}' not found`;\n   637→      events.emit({\n   638→        type: EventType.RUN_ERROR,\n   639→        message: errorMessage,\n   640→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   641→      });\n   642→      return { success: false, error: errorMessage };\n   643→    }\n   644→\n   645→    try {\n   646→      // Emit RUN_STARTED\n   647→      events.emit({\n   648→        type: EventType.RUN_STARTED,\n   649→        threadId,\n   650→        runId,\n   651→        input: JSON.stringify(inputs),\n   652→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   653→      });\n   654→\n   655→      // Call Dify API to trigger workflow\n   656→      const response = await fetch(`${this.apiBaseUrl}/workflows/run`, {\n   657→        method: 'POST',\n   658→        headers: {\n   659→          'Authorization': `Bearer ${apiKey}`,\n   660→          'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n   661→        },\n   662→        body: JSON.stringify({ inputs, response_mode: 'blocking', user: session.clientId }),\n   663→      });\n   664→\n   665→      const result = await response.json();\n   666→\n   667→      // Emit TEXT_MESSAGE events for workflow output\n   668→      events.emit({\n   669→        type: EventType.TEXT_MESSAGE_START,\n   670→        messageId: 'msg-1',\n   671→        role: 'assistant',\n   672→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   673→      });\n   674→\n   675→      events.emit({\n   676→        type: EventType.TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT,\n   677→        messageId: 'msg-1',\n   678→        delta: result.output,\n   679→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   680→      });\n   681→\n   682→      events.emit({\n   683→        type: EventType.TEXT_MESSAGE_END,\n   684→        messageId: 'msg-1',\n   685→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   686→      });\n   687→\n   688→      // Emit RUN_FINISHED\n   689→      events.emit({\n   690→        type: EventType.RUN_FINISHED,\n   691→        threadId,\n   692→        runId,\n   693→        result: JSON.stringify(result),\n   694→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   695→      });\n   696→\n   697→      return { success: true, outputs: result };\n   698→    } catch (error) {\n   699→      events.emit({\n   700→        type: EventType.RUN_ERROR,\n   701→        message: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unknown error',\n   702→        timestamp: Date.now(),\n   703→      });\n   704→\n   705→      return {\n   706→        success: false,\n   707→        error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unknown error',\n   708→      };\n   709→    }\n   710→  }\n   711→}\n   712→```\n   713→\n   714→**Key responsibilities:**\n   715→- Emit `RUN_STARTED` at the beginning\n   716→- Call your workflow platform's API\n   717→- Emit `TEXT_MESSAGE_*` events for workflow outputs\n   718→- Emit `TOOL_CALL_*` events if workflow needs to call frontend tools\n   719→- Wait for tool results via `session.pendingToolCalls` if needed\n   720→- Emit `RUN_FINISHED` on success or `RUN_ERROR` on failure\n   721→- Return workflow outputs or error\n   722→\n   723→#### Workflow Message Protocol\n   724→\n   725→Workflows use a use-ai-specific extension to the AG-UI protocol (not part of core AG-UI):\n   726→\n   727→**Client → Server:**\n   728→```typescript\n   729→{\n   730→  type: 'run_workflow',\n   731→  data: {\n   732→    runner: string;              // Runner name ('claude', 'dify', etc.)\n   733→    workflowId: string;          // Workflow identifier\n   734→    inputs: Record<string, any>; // Workflow inputs\n   735→    tools: ToolDefinition[];     // Available tools\n   736→    runId: string;               // Unique run ID\n   737→    threadId: string;            // Thread ID\n   738→  }\n   739→}\n   740→```\n   741→\n   742→**Server → Client:**\n   743→- Same AG-UI events as chat (`TEXT_MESSAGE_*`, `TOOL_CALL_*`, `RUN_*`)\n   744→- Workflows are stateless (no conversation history persistence)\n   745→\n   746→Location: `packages/server/src/types.ts` (UseAIClientMessage extends ClientMessage)\n   747→\n   748→#### Design Decisions\n   749→\n   750→**Why separate Agent and WorkflowRunner concepts?**\n   751→- Agents handle conversational chat with history persistence\n   752→- WorkflowRunners handle stateless, button-triggered operations\n   753→- Different lifecycle: multi-turn conversations vs single-run executions\n   754→- Different integrations: LLMs (Claude, GPT) vs workflow platforms (Dify, Flowise, n8n)\n   755→- Clear separation of concerns prevents confusion and simplifies implementation\n   756→\n   757→**Why separate hooks?**\n   758→- `useAI` is for conversational chat with UI\n   759→- `useAIWorkflow` is for headless background operations\n   760→- Clear separation of concerns and use cases\n   761→- Different UX patterns (chat vs button-triggered)\n   762→\n   763→**Why plugin architecture?**\n   764→- Extends server functionality without modifying core\n   765→- Allows third-party packages to add features\n   766→- Clean separation between core protocol and extensions\n   767→- Easy to add/remove functionality by installing/uninstalling plugins\n   768→- Encourages ecosystem development\n   769→\n   770→**Why keep AG-UI protocol pure?**\n   771→- `run_workflow` is a use-ai extension, not part of AG-UI core\n   772→- Keeps `@use-ai/core` 100% AG-UI compliant\n   773→- Extensions live in `@use-ai/server` types and plugins\n   774→- Enables potential future standardization\n   775→\n   776→**Why pluggable agents and workflow runners?**\n   777→- Enables integration with multiple AI platforms (Claude, GPT, Dify, Flowise, etc.)\n   778→- Separates platform-specific logic from server core\n   779→- Makes testing easier (mock agents/runners)\n   780→- Allows users to swap Claude for other LLMs\n   781→- Third-party workflow platforms can be integrated without modifying core\n   782→\n   783→### Tool Definition System\n   784→\n   785→Tools are defined using `defineTool()` with Zod schemas:\n   786→\n   787→```typescript\n   788→import { defineTool } from '@use-ai/client';\n   789→import { z } from 'zod';\n   790→\n   791→// With arguments\n   792→const addTodo = defineTool(\n   793→  'Add a new todo item to the list',\n   794→  z.object({ text: z.string() }),\n   795→  (input) => {\n   796→    // Implementation with typed input\n   797→    return { success: true, message: 'Todo added' };\n   798→  }\n   799→);\n   800→\n   801→// Without arguments\n   802→const logout = defineTool(\n   803→  'Log the current user out',\n   804→  () => { /* logout logic */ }\n   805→);\n   806→\n   807→// With confirmation required (dangerous actions)\n   808→const deleteAccount = defineTool(\n   809→  'Delete this user\\'s account permanently',\n   810→  () => { /* deletion logic */ },\n   811→  { confirmationRequired: true }\n   812→);\n   813→```\n   814→\n   815→**Under the hood:**\n   816→- Zod schema is converted to JSON Schema (via `zod-to-json-schema`) for Claude API\n   817→- Original Zod schema is retained for runtime validation\n   818→- `_execute()` method validates input with Zod before calling function\n   819→- TypeScript types are inferred from Zod schema\n   820→- `confirmationRequired` option aggressively prompts AI to ask for confirmation (not 100% foolproof)\n   821→\n   822→### Multi-Tool Use Support\n   823→\n   824→The library supports Claude's multi-tool use feature, enabling emergent \"bulk action\" behavior:\n   825→\n   826→```typescript\n   827→useAI({\n   828→  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n   829→  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n   830→});\n   831→```\n   832→\n   833→User: \"add a shopping list to make tonkotsu ramen\"\n   834→\n   835→Claude will automatically call `addTodo` multiple times for each ingredient, even though only single-item operations are defined. This is a powerful emergent behavior.\n   836→\n   837→### State Synchronization Pattern\n   838→\n   839→The example app demonstrates a pub/sub pattern for keeping UI in sync when tools mutate state:\n   840→\n   841→```typescript\n   842→const listeners = new Set<() => void>();\n   843→\n   844→export function subscribeTodos(listener: () => void) {\n   845→  listeners.add(listener);\n   846→  return () => listeners.delete(listener);\n   847→}\n   848→\n   849→function notifyListeners() {\n   850→  listeners.forEach(fn => fn());\n   851→}\n   852→\n   853→// Tools call notifyListeners() after state mutations\n   854→```\n   855→\n   856→This enables both AI operations and manual UI operations to update the same state.\n   857→\n   858→### Custom UI Support\n   859→\n   860→Default chat UI can be replaced with custom components:\n   861→\n   862→```typescript\n   863→<UseAIProvider\n   864→  serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8080\"\n   865→  CustomButton={MyCustomButton}  // Replace floating button\n   866→  CustomChat={MyCustomChat}      // Replace chat interface\n   867→>\n   868→  <App />\n   869→</UseAIProvider>\n   870→```\n   871→\n   872→### Chat History Persistence\n   873→\n   874→The library supports pluggable chat history persistence through the `ChatRepository` interface. By default, chats are stored in browser localStorage, but you can implement custom repositories for remote storage.\n   875→\n   876→#### Default Behavior (LocalStorage)\n   877→\n   878→Chat history is automatically persisted to localStorage.\n   879→\n   880→Features:\n   881→- **Auto-save**: Messages are automatically saved as they're sent/received\n   882→- **Auto-title generation**: Chat titles are automatically generated from the first user message (truncated to 50 characters)\n   883→- **Chat management UI**: Built-in buttons for creating new chats, viewing history, and clearing\n   884→- **Session persistence**: Chats survive page reloads and browser restarts\n   885→- **Smart initialization**: On page load, the most recent chat is automatically loaded; if no chats exist, a new one is created\n   886→- **Storage limit**: Only the last 20 chats are kept to avoid excessive localStorage usage. When creating a new chat, the oldest chat (by `updatedAt`) is automatically deleted if the limit is reached.\n   887→\n   888→#### Custom Repository (Remote Storage)\n   889→\n   890→Implement the `ChatRepository` interface for custom storage (e.g. remotely in your database).\n   891→See `packages/client/src/providers/chatRepository/types.ts`.\n   892→\n   893→#### Programmatic Access\n   894→\n   895→Access chat management functions from `useAIContext()`:\n   896→\n   897→```typescript\n   898→import { useAIContext } from '@use-ai/client';\n   899→\n   900→function MyComponent() {\n   901→  const {\n   902→    currentChatId,\n   903→    createNewChat,\n   904→    loadChat,\n   905→    deleteChat,\n   906→    listChats,\n   907→    clearCurrentChat,\n   908→  } = useAIContext();\n   909→\n   910→  const handleNewChat = async () => {\n   911→    const chatId = await createNewChat();\n   912→    console.log('Created new chat:', chatId);\n   913→  };\n   914→\n   915→  const handleLoadHistory = async () => {\n   916→    const chats = await listChats();\n   917→    console.log('Available chats:', chats);\n   918→  };\n   919→\n   920→  return (\n   921→    <div>\n   922→      <button onClick={handleNewChat}>New Chat</button>\n   923→      <button onClick={handleLoadHistory}>View History</button>\n   924→      <button onClick={clearCurrentChat}>Clear Current</button>\n   925→    </div>\n   926→  );\n   927→}\n   928→```\n   929→\n   930→## Observability\n   931→\n   932→### Langfuse Integration\n   933→\n   934→The server includes optional Langfuse integration for comprehensive LLM observability, cost tracking, and debugging. This integration leverages Vercel AI SDK's built-in telemetry support combined with Langfuse's OpenTelemetry processor.\n   935→\n   936→#### Setup\n   937→\n   938→1. Install optional dependencies (already in `optionalDependencies`):\n   939→```bash\n   940→bun install @langfuse/otel @opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node\n   941→```\n   942→\n   943→2. Configure environment variables:\n   944→```bash\n   945→export LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk-lf-...\n   946→export LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=sk-lf-...\n   947→export LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=https://cloud.langfuse.com  # Optional\n   948→```\n   949→\n   950→3. Start the server - Langfuse is automatically enabled if credentials are present\n   951→\n   952→#### What Gets Tracked\n   953→\n   954→When enabled, the server automatically tracks:\n   955→\n   956→- **Claude API calls**: Full request/response pairs with timing\n   957→- **Token usage**: Input, output, and total tokens per call\n   958→- **Tool calls**: Tool name, arguments, and execution results\n   959→- **Conversation history**: Multi-turn conversation traces\n   960→- **Session metadata**: Client ID, thread ID, run ID, IP address\n   961→- **Cost analytics**: Automatic cost calculation based on token usage\n   962→- **Latency**: Response time per API call and tool execution\n   963→- **Errors**: Exception tracking with full context\n   964→\n   965→#### Implementation Details\n   966→\n   967→The integration uses:\n   968→- `experimental_telemetry` flag in AI SDK's `generateText()` calls\n   969→- OpenTelemetry `NodeTracerProvider` for span collection\n   970→- `LangfuseSpanProcessor` for formatting and sending traces\n   971→- Automatic span creation via AI SDK's instrumentation\n   972→- Custom metadata attachment (session ID, tool count, etc.)\n   973→\n   974→See `packages/server/src/instrumentation.ts` for initialization logic.\n   975→\n   976→#### Benefits\n   977→\n   978→- Debug tool selection and execution issues\n   979→- Monitor token usage and costs across sessions\n   980→- Identify performance bottlenecks\n   981→- Track conversation patterns\n   982→- Analyze tool usage frequency\n   983→- Get alerts on errors or rate limits\n   984→\n   985→#### Optional Nature\n   986→\n   987→The integration is **completely optional**:\n   988→- Packages are `optionalDependencies` (won't fail if not installed)\n   989→- Dynamic `require()` prevents import errors\n   990→- Server runs normally without Langfuse credentials\n   991→- No code changes needed to disable (just unset env vars)\n   992→- Zero performance overhead when disabled\n   993→\n   994→## Security\n   995→\n   996→### Rate Limiting\n   997→- Configured via `RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS` and `RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS` env vars\n   998→- Rate limits by IP address prevent abuse of LLM credits\n   999→- Each IP address is rate-limited independently\n  1000→- Rate limits persist across Socket.IO connections from the same IP\n  1001→\n  1002→### \"Lethal Trifecta\" Mitigations\n  1003→Following [Simon Willison's security guidance](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/):\n  1004→\n  1005→1. **Limited internet access**: Server cannot invoke tools to access external internet (no curl, search, etc.)\n  1006→2. **No backend API access**: V1 cannot access backend APIs directly\n  1007→3. **Client-only scope**: AI can only see what's on the page and take actions users can already perform\n  1008→\n  1009→This prevents data exfiltration and limits attack surface.\n  1010→\n  1011→### Dangerous Actions\n  1012→Tools can use `confirmationRequired: true` option to aggressively prompt AI for user confirmation before execution. This is not 100% foolproof as AI may ignore prompts.\n  1013→\n  1014→### No Backend API Access (V1)\n  1015→Current version intentionally does not provide backend API integration to minimize security risks and complexity. All tools must be implemented in frontend code.\n  1016→\n  1017→## TypeScript Configuration\n  1018→\n  1019→- **Strict mode enabled**: All type checks enforced\n  1020→- **Module system**: ESM throughout (`\"type\": \"module\"` in all package.json files)\n  1021→- **Module resolution**: `\"bundler\"` (Bun-specific)\n  1022→- **Target**: ES2022\n  1023→- **Never use `any` type unless unavoidable**\n  1024→\n  1025→## Dependencies\n  1026→\n  1027→#