erify tool descriptions are clear and accurate\n  1150→3. Check if multi-tool use is causing unexpected behavior\n  1151→4. Review conversation history in server logs\n  1152→5. Test with E2E tests to reproduce consistently\n  1153→\n  1154→## Architecture Decisions\n  1155→\n  1156→### Why Client-Side Tool Execution?\n  1157→- Application state lives in React components\n  1158→- No need for state synchronization between frontend and backend\n  1159→- Direct access to browser APIs and local data\n  1160→- Simpler architecture with fewer moving parts\n  1161→- Better security (no backend API access in V1)\n  1162→\n  1163→### Why Socket.IO Instead of HTTP?\n  1164→- Real-time bidirectional communication needed for tool calls\n  1165→- Automatic reconnection with exponential backoff\n  1166→- Conversation history maintained per session\n  1167→- Lower latency for interactive chat experience\n  1168→- Easier to implement streaming responses\n  1169→- Transport fallbacks (WebSocket → long-polling) for compatibility\n  1170→- Built-in room and namespace support for future multi-tenant features\n  1171→\n  1172→### Why No Backend API Access in V1?\n  1173→- Reduces security risks (limits \"Lethal Trifecta\")\n  1174→- Simpler architecture and deployment\n  1175→- Forces developers to think about what AI can access\n  1176→- Sufficient for many use cases (UI manipulation, client state)\n  1177→\n  1178→### Why Zod for Tool Definitions?\n  1179→- Type safety at compile time\n  1180→- Runtime validation of LLM tool calls\n  1181→- Automatic JSON Schema generation for Claude API\n  1182→- Great developer experience with TypeScript integration\n  1183→\n  1184→## Code Organization\n  1185→\n  1186→- `packages/core/src/` - Core AG-UI protocol types and events\n  1187→- `packages/client/src/components/` - React UI components (chat, button)\n  1188→- `packages/client/src/hooks/` - React hooks (`useAI`, `useAIContext`)\n  1189→- `packages/client/src/utils/` - Utilities (`defineTo